\DOC REPEAT_N

\TYPE {REPEAT_N : int -> tactic -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{REPEAT_N n t} applies the tactic {t} exactly {n} times in sequence to the
goal.

\DESCRIBE
{n : int} is the number of applications and {t : tactic} is the tactic to be
iterated; the result is the goal obtained by running {t} on the goal {n} times
in succession, and in all observed traces a single subgoal comes out,
equivalent to {t THEN t THEN ... THEN t} with {n} copies. The cumulative effect
on conclusion and assumptions is just that of the {n} successive applications:
{
         A ?- t
   =============== REPEAT_N n t
         A' ?- t'
}
\noindent Here the conclusion may have {n} leading universal quantifiers
stripped, {n} disjuncts or antecedent layers removed, or be left unchanged
while the assumption list is rewritten; and the assumption list may gain
assumptions (for example implication antecedents moved in, or conjunctions
replaced by their components), lose assumptions ({n} of them discarded), or be
wholly replaced by an updated set. The iteration count appears both as a
literal integer and as a variable.

\COMMENTS
Since {t} is an arbitrary tactic, the observed traces show only that {t}'s
effect is iterated {n} times with one resulting goal each time.

\ENDDOC
